#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# A credential plugin that only ever fails, on purpose.
#
# It is referenced by the guard kubeconfig the entrypoint writes when
# cirrus-kubeconfig-init could not build a real one. The alternative is worse
# than it sounds: with NO file at $KUBECONFIG, client-go falls back to the pod's
# in-cluster service account, so kubectl silently authenticates as
# system:serviceaccount:<ns>:default and the user gets
#
#   Error from server (Forbidden): pods is forbidden: User
#   "system:serviceaccount:ood-ncote:default" cannot list resource "pods"
#
# which names an identity they have never heard of and says nothing about the
# actual problem -- their kubeconfig. Failing here instead puts the real
# explanation in front of them at the moment they run kubectl.

DOCS_URL="https://ncar-hpc-docs.readthedocs.io/en/latest/compute-systems/cirrus/guides/10-kubectl/kubectl/"

cat >&2 <<MSG

This session has no CIRRUS identity: the kubeconfig bootstrap did not complete,
so there is nothing to authenticate with.

  expected the CIRRUS kubeconfig at : ${CIRRUS_KUBECONFIG_SRC:-\$HOME/.kube/cirrus-config}
  session copy that was not written : ${KUBECONFIG:-<unset>}

To fix it:
  1. Check what you have in your GLADE home:
       ls -l "${HOME}/.kube/"
     This session prefers ~/.kube/cirrus-config, the container-friendly config:
     mlc1 and nwc1 contexts, Azure kubelogin, and the CA embedded in the file.
     ~/.kube/config is the Casper/Derecho one, which points at
     /etc/pki/tls/certs/cirrus-ca.crt and /opt/ncar/sbin/get-cirrus-token.sh --
     absolute paths that exist on those hosts and in no container.
  2. Re-run the bootstrap and read what it says:
       cirrus-kubeconfig-init
  3. If it is somewhere else, point the bootstrap at it:
       CIRRUS_KUBECONFIG_SRC=/path/to/config cirrus-kubeconfig-init

CIRRUS docs: ${DOCS_URL}

MSG
exit 1
